<title>USA: U.S. Q2 current account gap widened to $38.78 bln.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q2 current account gap widened to $38.78 bln.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. current account, seasonally adjusted, as reported by the Commerce Department.</p>
<p>In Billions of Dlrs/Credits:</p>
<p>    -		  Q2 1996    Q1'96--Rev from    Q4'95</p>
<p> Current Account    -38.78   -34.87   -35.59    -30.44</p>
<p> Goods Balance	-46.83   -42.73   -42.74    -38.03</p>
<p> Services Balance    18.96    18.50    18.39     18.64</p>
<p> Invest.Income Bal.  -1.60     0.26    -0.40     -1.89</p>
<p> Total Exports	257.52   252.65   252.92    250.13</p>
<p> Goods Exports	153.32   150.03   150.02    149.42</p>
<p> Services Exports    56.01    55.12    54.92     54.19</p>
<p>In Billions of Dlrs/Credits:</p>
<p>    -		  Q2 1996    Q1'96--Rev from    Q4'95</p>
<p> Total Imports     -287.00  -276.61  -277.66   -271.41</p>
<p> Goods Imports     -200.15  -192.76  -192.76   -187.45</p>
<p> Services Imports   -37.05   -36.62   -36.53    -35.56</p>
<p> Govt. Transfers     -9.30   -10.90   -10.84     -9.15</p>
<p> U.S.Assets Abroad  -49.17   -68.75   -55.73    -98.21</p>
<p> Foreign-HeldAssets  80.32    99.47    98.82     99.23</p>
<p>Net data--Govt. transfers, U.S. assets abroad and foreign-held assets.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast a second quarter current account deficit of $34.8 billion.</p>
<p>The department said net recorded capital inflows were $31.2 billion in the second quarter, compared with $30.7 billion in the first quarter. Acquisitions of foreign assets by U.S. residents and acquisitions of U.S. assets by foreign residents slowed by nearly equal amounts, it said.</p>
